{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Sections - Design-Test.md §4.5.
   ==========================================================================================

   A label on the iteration, so that a failure inside a table-driven test names the row:

       let &i = 0 :: Size
       while i < length(cases):
           section(cases[i])
           checkEqual(decode(encode(cases[i])), cases[i])
           i = i + 1

   No parameterized-test attribute, and no `beforeEach`. A table is a loop, and what a loop needs is
   this and nothing else.

   **A `lens`**, which is what makes it free: a function that hands control to a continuation exactly
   once, sharing the caller's stack. After inlining it is ordinary control flow - there is no
   closure, no allocation, and no capture rule to think about. Sections nest.

   > **One difference from §4.5, and it is the language's rather than this design's.** The section
   > gets no indented block: a lens call's continuation is *the rest of the enclosing block*, so
   > `section(label)` scopes to the end of the loop body it is written in rather than to a block
   > under it. That is the same shape `withLock` has, it is what every other lens in this tree reads
   > like, and the alternative would be a block form the parser does not have.

   The labels are one joined `String` rather than a stack of them, for the reason Report.yana gives:
   a global's initializer needs a constant form, and `""` is one where `[] :: [String]` is not. The
   join is what a report line wants anyway, and the restore is the saved copy - one allocation per
   entered section, which is a cost a table of a hundred rows pays a hundred times and nothing else
   pays at all.
-}

pub let &sectionLabels = "" :: String

pub lens fn section(label: String) -> {}:
    let saved = copy(sectionLabels)

    if length(sectionLabels) > (0 :: Size) then sectionLabels ++= " / "
    sectionLabels ++= "{label}"

    yield {}

    -- On the way out, whatever happened inside. A `return` from the middle of a section is an early
    -- exit from the *test*, and a lens's leaving path is exactly what runs on one - which is the
    -- reason this is a lens rather than a function taking a body.
    sectionLabels = saved
